**Answer: True.** The only irreducible representation of a $p$-group over a field of characteristic $p$ is the trivial representation.

**Proof.**

Let $G$ be a $p$-group, $|G|=p^n$, and let $\mathbb{K}$ be a field with $\operatorname{char}(\mathbb{K})=p$. Let $\rho:G\to GL(V)$ be an irreducible representation, where $V$ is a finite-dimensional $\mathbb{K}$-vector space. We prove by induction on $|G|$ that $\rho$ is trivial.

**Base case.** If $|G|=1$, the claim is immediate.

**Inductive step.** Assume $|G|>1$. A standard fact about $p$-groups is that the center $Z(G)$ is nontrivial. Pick any $z\in Z(G)$, $z\neq e$. Since $G$ is a $p$-group, $z$ has order $p^{k}$ for some $k\geq 1$. Consider the linear operator $\rho(z)\in GL(V)$. It satisfies
$$\rho(z)^{p^{k}}=\rho(z^{p^{k}})=\rho(e)=I.$$
Over a field of characteristic $p$, we have the polynomial identity
$$x^{p^{k}}-1=(x-1)^{p^{k}}.$$
Hence the minimal polynomial of $\rho(z)$ divides $(x-1)^{p^{k}}$, which means $\rho(z)-I$ is nilpotent. In particular, $1$ is the only eigenvalue of $\rho(z)$, so the fixed-point subspace
$$V^{z}:=\{v\in V:\rho(z)v=v\}=\ker(\rho(z)-I)$$
is nonzero.

Since $z\in Z(G)$ is central, for any $g\in G$ and $v\in V^{z}$,
$$\rho(z)\rho(g)v=\rho(zg)v=\rho(gz)v=\rho(g)\rho(z)v=\rho(g)v,$$
so $V^{z}$ is $G$-invariant. By irreducibility of $\rho$, we conclude $V^{z}=V$, i.e., $z$ acts trivially on $V$.

This holds for every $z\in Z(G)$, so the entire center $Z(G)$ acts trivially. Therefore $\rho$ factors through $G/Z(G)$, giving an irreducible representation $\bar{\rho}:G/Z(G)\to GL(V)$ over $\mathbb{K}$.

Since $Z(G)$ is nontrivial, $|G/Z(G)|<|G|$, and $G/Z(G)$ is again a $p$-group (or trivial). By the inductive hypothesis, $\bar{\rho}$ is trivial: every coset $gZ(G)$ acts as the identity on $V$. Hence every $g\in G$ acts as the identity on $V$.

Therefore $\rho$ is the trivial representation. $\blacksquare$

